<div class="poster">

  <!-- ═══ HEADER ═══ -->
  <header class="header">
    <div class="eyebrow">Data Report &bull; A1 Poster</div>
    <h1>The History of Books<span class="ampersand">:</span><br>From Manuscripts to Mass Production</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">How book production evolved from rare handwritten manuscripts to millions of printed titles across 1,500 years — and what this reveals about literacy, wealth, and culture.</p>
  </header>
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- ═══ KEY METRICS ═══ -->
  <div class="metrics-bar">
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">~1440</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Gutenberg Press</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">10 Centuries</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Manuscript Data</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">1500–2009</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Printed Book Span</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">&gt;49 Pages</div>
      <div class="metric-label">UNESCO Definition</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">&gt;500 Years</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Print Era Coverage</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 1: THE MANUSCRIPT ERA ═══ -->
  <h2 class="section-title">The Manuscript Era (6th–15th Century)</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Before the printing press, every book was copied by hand — a slow, expensive process. Production grew gradually over nearly a millennium, driven by monastic scriptoria, rising universities, and expanding elite literacy. Books remained scarce luxury goods accessible only to the wealthy.</p>

  <!-- ═══ MAIN TIMELINE ═══ -->
  <div class="viz-container">
    <div class="viz-label">The Long Arc of Book Production &bull; 6th Century → Present Day</div>
    <div id="main-timeline"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ GUTENBERG CALLOUT ═══ -->
  <div class="gutenberg-callout">
    <div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <h3>The Gutenberg Revolution</h3>
      <p>Around 1440, movable-type printing transformed book production from a craft into an industry. Books went from rare, hand-copied artifacts to widely available commodities — fueling the Renaissance, the Reformation, and the Scientific Revolution. It remains one of the most consequential inventions in human history.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout-stat">
      <div class="stat-num">~1440</div>
      <div class="stat-label">The Inflection Point</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 2: PRINT ERA + TWO COLUMNS ═══ -->
  <h2 class="section-title">The Printed Book Era (1450–Present)</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">After Gutenberg, printed book output exploded. Each period brought dramatic expansion — from the first printed volumes to industrial mass production and today's global publishing industry.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col">
      <div class="viz-container">
        <div class="viz-label">Printed Book Output Growth by Period</div>
        <div id="print-growth-chart"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <div class="mirror-section" style="height:100%;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;margin-bottom:14px;color:#1a1008;">Key Eras of Expansion</h3>
        <div class="era-list">
          <div class="era-item">
            <div class="era-num">1</div>
            <div><strong>Incunabula Period (1450–1500)</strong><br><span class="detail">First printed books spread from Mainz across Europe; ~30,000 editions produced.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-item">
            <div class="era-num">2</div>
            <div><strong>Reformation & Renaissance (1500–1600)</strong><br><span class="detail">Presses established in >200 European cities; secular works begin to grow.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-item">
            <div class="era-num">3</div>
            <div><strong>Enlightenment (1700–1800)</strong><br><span class="detail">Scientific journals, encyclopedias, and novels drive explosive growth.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-item">
            <div class="era-num">4</div>
            <div><strong>Industrial Era (1800–1950)</strong><br><span class="detail">Steam presses, rail distribution, and rising literacy create a mass market.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-item">
            <div class="era-num">5</div>
            <div><strong>Global Publishing (1950–Present)</strong><br><span class="detail">Digital technology and emerging-market readership reshape the landscape.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 3: MODERN PUBLISHING ═══ -->
  <h2 class="section-title">Modern Book Publishing &amp; Consumption</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Per-capita book publishing varies enormously across countries — reflecting education, income, and cultural reading habits. Data from Fink-Jensen (2015, IISH) tracks new book titles per million people from 1500 to 2009.</p>

  <div class="viz-container">
    <div class="viz-label">New Book Titles Per Million People — Selected Countries (Recent Year)</div>
    <div id="modern-chart"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 4: BOOKS AS A MIRROR ═══ -->
  <h2 class="section-title">Books as a Mirror of Society</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Book production is one of the most powerful proxies for human development. The volume and nature of book output reflects deeper currents of civilization across centuries and continents.</p>

  <div class="mirror-section">
    <div class="mirror-grid">
      <div class="mirror-card">
        <div class="mirror-icon">📖</div>
        <h4>Literacy</h4>
        <p>Higher book output correlates with population literacy. As books became cheaper, reading spread from elites to the masses.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mirror-card">
        <div class="mirror-icon">💰</div>
        <h4>Income &amp; Wealth</h4>
        <p>Affordable books signal rising prosperity. The shift from luxury manuscripts to cheap paperbacks tracks economic development.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mirror-card">
        <div class="mirror-icon">🎓</div>
        <h4>Education</h4>
        <p>Expanding school systems drive demand for textbooks. University growth coincided with book booms in the 12th and 19th centuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mirror-card">
        <div class="mirror-icon">⛪</div>
        <h4>Religion &amp; Culture</h4>
        <p>Religious texts dominated early output. Over centuries, secular works — science, fiction, philosophy — claimed the majority.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ CLOSING QUOTE ═══ -->
  <div class="closing-quote">
    <p style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;font-style:italic;color:#5c4a33;line-height:1.5;">"The transition of books — from elite luxury to mass commodity — is one of the most important stories in the history of human knowledge."</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ FOOTER ═══ -->
  <div class="footer">
    <div class="source-col">
      <strong>Data Sources:</strong> Buringh, E. &amp; Van Zanden, J.L. (2009). "Charting the Rise of the West: Manuscripts and Printed Books in Europe." <em>The Journal of Economic History</em>, 69(2), 409–445. &bull; Fink-Jensen, J. (2015). IISH Data Collection. &bull; Roser, M. &amp; Ritchie, H. (2023). "Books." <em>Our World in Data</em>. Licensed under CC BY.
    </div>
    <div class="brand-col">
      <div class="brand-name">The History of Books</div>
      <div>Data Report Poster &bull; May 2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<script src="/app/vendor/infographic.min.js"></script>
<script>
