 "-    font-size: 11px;", "+    font-size: 10px;", "     color: #8a7560;", "   }", "-  .footer .source-col { max-width: 600px; line-height: 1.6; }", "+  .footer .source-col { max-width: 680px; line-height: 1.5; }", "   .footer .brand-col { text-align: right; }", "   .footer .brand-name {", "     font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;", "-    font-size: 16px;", "+    font-size: 15px;", "     font-weight: 700;", "     color: #5c4a33;", "   }"]}, {"oldStart": 287, "oldLines": 7, "newStart": 331, "newLines": 7, "lines": ["   <header class="header">", "     <div class="eyebrow">Data Report &bull; A1 Poster</div>", "     <h1>The History of Books<span class="ampersand">:</span><br>From Manuscripts to Mass Production</h1>", "-    <p class="subtitle">A data-driven look at how book production evolved from rare handwritten manuscripts to millions of printed titles across 1,500 years — and what this reveals about literacy, wealth, and culture.</p>", "+    <p class="subtitle">How book production evolved from rare handwritten manuscripts to millions of printed titles across 1,500 years — and what this reveals about literacy, wealth, and culture.</p>", "   </header>", "   <div class="divider"></div>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 295, "oldLines": 19, "newStart": 339, "newLines": 19, "lines": ["   <div class="metrics-bar">", "     <div class="metric-item">", "       <div class="metric-value">~1440</div>", "-      <div class="metric-label">Gutenberg's Printing Press</div>", "+      <div class="metric-label">Gutenberg Press</div>", "     </div>", "     <div class="metric-item">", "       <div class="metric-value">10 Centuries</div>", "-      <div class="metric-label">Manuscript Data Span</div>", "+      <div class="metric-label">Manuscript Data</div>", "     </div>", "     <div class="metric-item">", "       <div class="metric-value">1500–2009</div>", "-      <div class="metric-label">Printed Book Data</div>", "+      <div class="metric-label">Printed Book Span</div>", "     </div>", "     <div class="metric-item">", "       <div class="metric-value">&gt;49 Pages</div>", "-      <div class="metric-label">UNESCO Book Definition</div>", "+      <div class="metric-label">UNESCO Definition</div>", "     </div>", "     <div class="metric-item">", "       <div class="metric-value">&gt;500 Years</div>"]}, {"oldStart": 317, "oldLines": 11, "newStart": 361, "newLines": 11, "lines": [" ", "   <!-- ═══ SECTION 1: THE MANUSCRIPT ERA ═══ -->", "   <h2 class="section-title">The Manuscript Era (6th–15th Century)</h2>", "-  <p class="section-desc">Before the printing press, every book was copied by hand — a slow, expensive process that made written works scarce luxury goods. Production grew gradually over nearly a millennium, driven by religious institutions, rising universities, and expanding elite literacy.</p>", "+  <p class="section-desc">Before the printing press, every book was copied by hand — a slow, expensive process. Production grew gradually over nearly a millennium, driven by monastic scriptoria, rising universities, and expanding elite literacy. Books remained scarce luxury goods accessible only to the wealthy.</p>", " ", "-  <!-- ═══ MAIN TIMELINE VISUALIZATION ═══ -->", "+  <!-- ═══ MAIN TIMELINE ═══ -->", "   <div class="viz-container">", "-    <div class="viz-label">The Long Arc of Book Production &bull; 6th Century to Present</div>", "+    <div class="viz-label">The Long Arc of Book Production &bull; 6th Century → Present Day</div>", "     <div id="main-timeline"></div>", "   </div>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 330, "oldLines": 48, "newStart": 374, "newLines": 48, "lines": ["     <div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>", "     <div class="callout-body">", "       <h3>The Gutenberg Revolution</h3>", "-      <p>Around 1440, Johannes Gutenberg's movable-type printing press transformed book production from a craft into an industry. Books went from rare, hand-copied artifacts to widely available commodities — fueling the Renaissance, the Reformation, and the Scientific Revolution. The printing press is widely regarded as one of the most consequential inventions in human history.</p>", "+      <p>Around 1440, movable-type printing transformed book production from a craft into an industry. Books went from rare, hand-copied artifacts to widely available commodities — fueling the Renaissance, the Reformation, and the Scientific Revolution. It remains one of the most consequential inventions in human history.</p>", "     </div>", "     <div class="callout-stat">", "       <div class="stat-num">~1440</div>", "-      <div class="stat-label">Inflection Point</div>", "+      <div class="stat-label">The Inflection Point</div>", "     </div>", "   </div>", " ", "-  <!-- ═══ SECTION 2: PRINTED BOOKS GROWTH ═══ -->", "+  <!-- ═══ SECTION 2: PRINT ERA + TWO COLUMNS ═══ -->", "   <h2 class="section-title">The Printed Book Era (1450–Present)</h2>", "-  <p class="section-desc">After Gutenberg, printed book output exploded. Each half-century brought dramatic expansion: from the first printed volumes spreading across Western Europe, through the Enlightenment's publishing boom, to the industrial-scale mass production of the 19th and 20th centuries.</p>", "+  <p class="section-desc">After Gutenberg, printed book output exploded. Each period brought dramatic expansion — from the first printed volumes to industrial mass production and today's global publishing industry.</p>", " ", "   <div class="two-col">", "     <div class="col">", "-      <div class="viz-container" style="padding-bottom: 22px;">", "-        <div class="viz-label">Printed Books Output by Half-Century</div>", "-        <div id="print-growth-chart" style="width:100%;height:320px;"></div>", "+      <div class="viz-container">", "+        <div class="viz-label">Printed Book Output Growth by Period</div>", "+        <div id="print-growth-chart"></div>", "       </div>", "     </div>", "     <div class="col">", "       <div class="mirror-section" style="height:100%;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">", "-        <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:22px;margin-bottom:12px;">Key Eras of Expansion</h3>", "-        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;">", "-          <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;">", "-            <span style="background:#c4562c;color:#fff;border-radius:50%;width:24px;height:24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;">1</span>", "-            <div><strong style="color:#1a1008;">Incunabula Period (1450–1500)</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#6b5a44;">First printed books spread rapidly from Mainz across Europe; ~30,000 editions produced.</span></div>", "+        <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;margin-bottom:14px;color:#1a1008;">Key Eras of Expansion</h3>", "+        <div class="era-list">", "+          <div class="era-item">", "+            <div class="era-num">1</div>", "+            <div><strong>Incunabula Period (1450–1500)</strong><br><span class="detail">First printed books spread from Mainz across Europe; ~30,000 editions produced.</span></div>", "           </div>", "-          <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;">", "-            <span style="background:#c4562c;color:#fff;border-radius:50%;width:24px;height:24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;">2</span>", "-            <div><strong style="color:#1a1008;">Reformation &amp; Renaissance (1500–1600)</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#6b5a44;">Printing presses established in &gt;200 European cities; religious texts dominate but secular works grow.</span></div>", "+          <div class="era-item">", "+            <div class="era-num">2</div>", "+            <div><strong>Reformation & Renaissance (1500–1600)</strong><br><span class="detail">Presses established in >200 European cities; secular works begin to grow.</span></div>", "           </div>", "-          <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;">", "-            <span style="background:#c4562c;color:#fff;border-radius:50%;width:24px;height:24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;">3</span>", "-            <div><strong style="color:#1a1008;">Enlightenment (1700–1800)</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#6b5a44;">Scientific journals, encyclopedias, and novels drive explosive growth in both output and readership.</span></div>", "+          <div class="era-item">", "+            <div class="era-num">3</div>", "+            <div><strong>Enlightenment (1700–1800)</strong><br><span class="detail">Scientific journals, encyclopedias, and novels drive explosive growth.</span></div>", "           </div>", "-          <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;">", "-            <span style="background:#c4562c;color:#fff;border-radius:50%;width:24px;height:24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;">4</span>", "-            <div><strong style="color:#1a1008;">Industrial Era (1800–1950)</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#6b5a44;">Steam-powered presses, railways for distribution, and rising literacy create a mass market for books.</span></div>", "+          <div class="era-item">", "+            <div class="era-num">4</div>", "+            <div><strong>Industrial Era (1800–1950)</strong><br><span class="detail">Steam presses, rail distribution, and rising literacy create a mass market.</span></div>", "           </div>", "-          <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;">", "-            <span style="background:#c4562c;color:#fff;border-radius:50%;width:24px;height:24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;">5</span>", "-            <div><strong style="color:#1a1008;">Global Publishing (1950–Present)</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#6b5a44;">Digital technology, global distribution, and emerging-market readership reshape the publishing landscape.</span></div>", "+          <div class="era-item">", "+            <div class="era-num">5</div>", "+            <div><strong>Global Publishing (1950–Present)</strong><br><span class="detail">Digital technology and emerging-market readership reshape the landscape.</span></div>", "           </div>", "         </div>", "       </div>"]}, {"oldStart": 380, "oldLines": 7, "newStart": 424, "newLines": 7, "lines": [" ", "   <!-- ═══ SECTION 3: MODERN PUBLISHING ═══ -->", "   <h2 class="section-title">Modern Book Publishing &amp; Consumption</h2>", "-  <p class="section-desc">In the modern era, per-capita book publishing varies enormously across countries — reflecting differences in education systems, income levels, and cultural reading habits. Data from Jonathan Fink-Jensen (2015, IISH) tracks new book titles per million people from 1500 to 2009.</p>", "+  <p class="section-desc">Per-capita book publishing varies enormously across countries — reflecting education, income, and cultural reading habits. Data from Fink-Jensen (2015, IISH) tracks new book titles per million people from 1500 to 2009.</p>", " ", "   <div class="viz-container">", "     <div class="viz-label">New Book Titles Per Million People — Selected Countries (Recent Year)</div>"]}, {"oldStart": 389, "oldLines": 34, "newStart": 433, "newLines": 36, "lines": [" ", "   <!-- ═══ SECTION 4: BOOKS AS A MIRROR ═══ -->", "   <h2 class="section-title">Books as a Mirror of Society</h2>", "-  <p class="section-desc">Book production is one of the most powerful proxies for human development. Across centuries and continents, the volume and nature of book output reflects the deeper currents of civilization.</p>", "+  <p class="section-desc">Book production is one of the most powerful proxies for human development. The volume and nature of book output reflects deeper currents of civilization across centuries and continents.</p>", " ", "-  <div class="mirror-grid">", "-    <div class="mirror-card">", "-      <div class="mirror-icon">📖</div>", "-      <h4>Literacy</h4>", "-      <p>Higher book output strongly correlates with population literacy rates. As books became cheaper, reading spread from elites to the masses.</p>", "+  <div class="mirror-section">", "+    <div class="mirror-grid">", "+      <div class="mirror-card">", "+        <div class="mirror-icon">📖</div>", "+        <h4>Literacy</h4>", "+        <p>Higher book output correlates with population literacy. As books became cheaper, reading spread from elites to the masses.</p>", "+      </div>", "+      <div class="mirror-card">", "+        <div class="mirror-icon">💰</div>", "+        <h4>Income &amp; Wealth</h4>", "+        <p>Affordable books signal rising prosperity. The shift from luxury manuscripts to cheap paperbacks tracks economic development.</p>", "+      </div>", "+      <div class="mirror-card">", "+        <div class="mirror-icon">🎓</div>", "+        <h4>Education</h4>", "+        <p>Expanding school systems drive demand for textbooks. University growth coincided with book booms in the 12th and 19th centuries.</p>", "+      </div>", "+      <div class="mirror-card">", "+        <div class="mirror-icon">⛪</div>", "+        <h4>Religion &amp; Culture</h4>", "+        <p>Religious texts dominated early output. Over centuries, secular works — science, fiction, philosophy — claimed the majority.</p>", "+      </div>", "     </div>", "-    <div class="mirror-card">", "-      <div class="mirror-icon">💰</div>", "-      <h4>Income &amp; Wealth</h4>", "-      <p>Affordable books are a signal of rising prosperity. The transition from luxury manuscripts to cheap paperbacks tracks economic development.</p>", "-    </div>", "-    <div class="mirror-card">", "-      <div class="mirror-icon">🎓</div>", "-      <h4>Education</h4>", "-      <p>Expanding school systems create demand for textbooks and academic works. University growth in the 12th and 19th centuries coincided with book booms.</p>", "-    </div>", "-    <div class="mirror-card">", "-      <div class="mirror-icon">⛪</div>", "-      <h4>Religion &amp; Culture</h4>", "-      <p>Religious texts dominated early manuscript and print output. Over centuries, secular works — science, fiction, philosophy — grew to claim the majority share.</p>", "-    </div>", "   </div>", " ", "   <!-- ═══ CLOSING QUOTE ═══ -->", "-  <div style="text-align:center;padding:24px 40px;margin-bottom:40px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 0%,#f5efe0 50%,transparent 100%);">", "-    <p style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:22px;font-style:italic;color:#5c4a33;line-height:1.5;">"The transition of books — from elite luxury to mass commodity — is one of the most important stories in the history of human knowledge."</p>", "+  <div class="closing-quote">", "+    <p style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;font-style:italic;color:#5c4a33;line-height:1.5;">"The transition of books — from elite luxury to mass commodity — is one of the most important stories in the history of human knowledge."</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <!-- ═══ FOOTER ═══ -->"]}, {"oldStart": 434, "oldLines": 45, "newStart": 480, "newLines": 43, "lines": [" ", " <script src="/app/vendor/infographic.min.js"></script>", " <script>", "-// ── Main Timeline: Manuscript Era → Printed Books ──", " const timelineSyntax = `infographic sequence-timeline-rounded-rect-node", " data", "   title The Long Arc of Book Production", "   desc From rare handwritten manuscripts to billions of printed books across 1,500 years", "   sequences", "     - label 6th C.", "-      desc Minimal manuscript output in Europe — books are rare luxury items", "+      desc Minimal manuscript output — books are rare luxury items", "     - label 9th C.", "-      desc Carolingian Renais